Mitch <gmitchel@garnet.acns.fsu.edu> wrote:

# Well then, it sounds like the opponents of censorship need to give the
# system administrators more flak than those who favor censorship.  Try
# to get a few system administrators fired for interfering with academic
# freedom.  Stage a few acts of civil disobedience, like overload mail
# servers, etc.

I suggest that the aforementioned opponents of censorship vote with their
wallets/pocketbooks and get an account at a commercial service provider
or public access system.  It's not censorship if a newspaper isn't sold
or handed out on your block, but is on every other block around you.
If your institution's system administration staff is rightfully concerned
about the disk space vanishing into the growing black hole of alt groups,
or about the civil and criminal exposure that can result from the contents
of some newsgroups, then they are >doing their jobs< properly.  They are
protecting the computing environment, and its managers and owners, from
interruptions in service.  Do not urge irresponsible acts like swamping
mail servers.  Be a mature, responsible role model.  If you can't get
everything that you want as a condition of employment, negotiate the cost
of an account at world or zilker or any other service provider at your
next salary review.  If you can't get that, either bite the bullet and
pay for it out of what you're getting now, or accept that net access is
a discretionary expenditure that's not in your budget.  After all, if
you can't or don't wish to pay for a pro-rated part of a network node's
operation and support, how can you demand that your employer foot the
bill for the whole thing, and run it exactly the way that you want?
It's a frill, perhaps a benefit, but by no means a guaranteed right.

# You cannot drop Usenet groups on the basis of their viewpoint.  If you
# can no longer handle the load and need to drop Usenet groups, you have to
# do it based on criteria other than viewpoint.  Period.

I believe that this is what your previous correspondent was doing.  They
were making a pragmatic evaluation over whether their current resources
were adequate to handle certain high-traffic groups.  Secondary in their
mind may have been the likelihood of certain groups resulting in civil
legal exposure, either in copyright violation or sexual harassment
suits.  How many people propounding freedom of expression will take a
pay cut to allow their employer to mass a defense to such suits, and
to pay off the lawyers and the plaintiff(s) if the employer loses?

# Not at all the same thing.  No one else has to look at his screen.  There
# are many other places they can avert their eyes to.

If I may be old-fashioned for a moment, what academic purpose is being
served by viewing said unclothed image on a scholastic computer resource?
As it is conceivable that some of what is viewed is sexually stimulating,
should it be permitted for users of this particular lab to manually
stimulate themselves (or, heh heh, others?)

# The intolerant few who try to restrict what others
# can research and discuss are the problem.  If you're going to insist
# that someone leave, punish those who are actively interfering with
# free inquiry and free expression.

We aren't talking about people arguing over who's got the "better"
cure for cancer here.  It may be possible that the lab was set up to
permit students to work on that between-Usenet filler called course-
work.  Preserving this environment, and drawing the line when a perq
like Usenet takes over too much of the allocated disk space and CPU
time, is someone's job.  You can perhaps make their lives easier.
Pay, or chip in with as many others as can be convinced (or get a
grant) to supplement the probably-stretched resources with an extra
server, some disk space, and any required network hardware.  That is
the price of free expression, and in this case it's not an arbitrary
number:  It's the cost of the technology, plus any additional support
staff, plus any liability insurance to defend against legal actions by
those not approving of the choices made in permitting free expression.

# By why are you not crusading to eliminate all of the other .GIFs from
# your system in the name of efficient resource usage, mission-specific
# computing, and an overloaded server?  Why are you so preoccupied with
# the naked .GIFs?

One machine that I administer received a full Usenet feed, with all the
alt.binaries groups present and accounted for.  The daily traffic was
between sixty and eighty megabytes on the average.  When we changed to
a site that provided the same feed without the alt.sex.pictures and
alt.binaries.pictures.erotica hierarchies, we shed about thirty megabytes
or so per day.  Other people's experiences may vary.

# The only groups you have suggested eliminating in your messages to me are
# the naked .GIFs and the alt.sex.xxxx groups.  You haven't mentioned the
# other alt.xxxx groups, the rec.xxxx groups, the misc.xxxx groups, etc.

In this case, I would say that the cup is definitely three-quarters full
and not one-quarter empty.  There is a resource problem, and a potential
legal problem, eith some groups, but the majority are being left alone.
If you want to read those few excluded groups, pay for access to them.

# By imposing censorship, you make yourself responsible for every message
# posted on Usenet at your site.

This is a legitimate point, if I interpreted it correctly as "every
message that passes through and is stored at your site."  The criteria
must be fairly defined.  Alas, raising it can be walking a thin line
between the news that you have now and no news at all.  The responsibility
for articles passing through that site is considerably lessened when that
number of articles is zero.

# I will tell you again, you make yourself liable for everything on Usenet
# at your site, if you engage in censorship.

This might be a viable defense for someone, someday.  Right now, with a
relative paucity of computer systems that are tariffed common carriers,
it is sophistry.  Inspect it all or not, if someone reads it and sees
something that offends or violates applicable law, a defense from civil
or criminal legal action may be necessary.

# And so why are you not on a mission to recover disk space from these
# contraband postings as well?  Why just the nude postings?

Keep baiting them...  How much space is dedicated to news at your school?
How much would the typical user's disk quota be increased if the news
spool space were freed up for user accounts?  Hey, a cost effective
solution that stops all this grousing about news we've been hearing!

Bob
