A double standard for computer material at the U. of Houston ...

sears@uh.edu (Paul S. Sears) writes:

[...]
>We haven't had a case here about carrying groups that violate sexual  
>harassment, but we have had complaints filed about users viewing "explicit"  
>images on University Computers.  The people who have filed complaints felt  
>offended by these images and filed complaints under UH's Sexual Harassment  
>policy in that it "creates an intimidating, hostile, or offensive university  
>environment."
>
>Students may view these images on their personal (non UH property)  
>computers, but they are not allowed to view them in a _public_ computing  
>facility (unless it is for a class and they need to have written  
>authorization).
[...]

Does the same rule apply to looking at nude images in the Univesity
Library?  If not, why the double standard?

Every sexual harassment rule I've ever read, requires a hearing. It
also requires that someone actually be harassed.

Your special-for-computer-labs rule apparently says that a person
sitting alone in a computer room (or away from everyone else) who
looks at Salvador Dali's shocking "Young Virgin Autosodomised by her
own Chastity"
    <a href="http://mistral.enst.fr/~pioch/louvre/paintings/dali/violence/dali.virgin.jpg">Virgin</a>.
is automatically guilty of harassment.

As the U. of Delaware has recently beening find in court, there is a
fine line between legal-harassment-prevention and censorship. I think
the U. of Houston may have stepped over the line.

A rule banning "explicit-material" in public labs is unnecessary
because university computer facilities can (and should) be treated as
any other university facility. That means banning the act of
harassment, not the materials that can be used to harass but that can
also be used without harassing anyone. At least in the U.S., virtually
every university has a sexual harassment policy that not only covers
harassment via computers but that also dictates the exact procedure
for handling sexual harassment complaints. (Having a procedure is
important because the line between constitutionally protected
expression and unprotected expression is dim and uncertain.) Computer
sites should publicize the university's sexual harassment rules; they
should not try to preempt them. See the referenced U. of Illinois
report on the Status of Women for concrete suggestions on publicizing
your existing sexual harassment rules.

